<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1998"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:webmaster@catchbtk.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Tom_voigt<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tom_voigt<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, December 16, 2004 - 3:43 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text--><a href="http://www.catchbtk.com/discus/messages/1/16.html?1103238537"> <b>Continued from here</b></a>.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1998-->
<!--Post: 2010--><!--Time: 1103244319-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2010"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Jim44095<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Jim44095<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, December 16, 2004 - 4:45 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->How did he know that the Otero daughter would be home from school that day?Did he see the father drop off the other 2 or did he take the call from the mother calling them off?This has always bothered me,I think the daughter was the target in the first place.Any insight you can give Mike would be appreciated.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2010-->
<!--Post: 2011--><!--Time: 1103244500-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2011"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:webmaster@catchbtk.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Tom_voigt<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tom_voigt<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, December 16, 2004 - 4:48 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Please reserve comments in this thread to Kelleher&#39;s profile.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2011-->
<!--Post: 2023--><!--Time: 1103253807-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2023"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Mike_kelleher<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Mike_kelleher<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, December 16, 2004 - 7:23 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Jim: <BR> <BR>It&#39;s impossible to say. However, it is very likely that BTK had spent some time observing the family before the attack. In fact, he may have known their movements and habits well. It&#39;s not unusual for a serial killer to strike close to home at the early stages of his career. In fact, it&#39;s not all that unusual for the killer to know his first victim&#40;s&#41; in some way. If I were just walking into the case, I would want to know as much as possible about the Otero attack since it would likely yield the best profile of the killer. <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2023-->
<!--Post: 2028--><!--Time: 1103256969-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2028"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Q<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Q<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, December 16, 2004 - 8:16 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Mike, <BR>In regards to your comment above &#34;I would want to know as much as possible about the Otero attack.&#34; <BR> <BR>What&#39;s your take on Charlie Otero&#39;s statements about his fathers strange behavior for &#34;days&#34; leading up to the day of the crime? <BR> <BR>It would seem to me that the father is the key to focus on if he was &#34;expecting&#34; something he feared that much.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2028-->
<!--Post: 2041--><!--Time: 1103262830-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2041"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Mike_kelleher<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Mike_kelleher<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, December 16, 2004 - 9:53 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Q: <BR> <BR>Hard to say. It could be memory-filling, which is common after a violent incident. It&#39;s the type of behavior that represents a &#34;searching&#34; or &#34;rationale&#34; for why the incident happened. In other words, looking for reason where there is no reason. Of course, it could also be true, in which case Otero was fearful for some reason, which could also make sense. With the lack of any other pertinent information, I would tend to go with memory filling as the safest course. We all do it and it&#39;s a very understandable reaction to that kind of trauma. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2041-->
<!--Post: 2154--><!--Time: 1103340795-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2154"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Q<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Q<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, December 17, 2004 - 7:33 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Mike, I&#39;m not sure I buy into the memory filling theory. <BR> <BR>The things Charlie Otero speaks of are quite specific, such as, his father having him look out the window for a company vehicle &#40;which is interesting that he would have his son do it and the putting them all in the closet. <BR> <BR>On the other hand the idea that his Dad did not protect him &#40;sending him to look&#41; and also did &#40;hiding them&#41; does fill the need for some resolution, albeit temporary in all likely hood, of the anger he must have had/has? <BR> <BR>Do you agree? <BR> <BR>Charlie Otero claims to not have been interviewed since the 70&#39;s if I remember. How thorough is that? The terror this guy has lived with surely must feed BTKs ego. The Anna why letter and the general aspect of the fear or &#34;terrorism&#34; is certainly a source for ego gratification for this Murderer. Do you see these aspects as seperate games/interests for him or all part of one big sick twisted plan? <BR> <BR>Do you know what happend to Shirley Vians Kids?  <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2154-->
<!--Post: 2166--><!--Time: 1103344814-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2166"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Oklahoma_mike<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Oklahoma_mike<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->11-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, December 17, 2004 - 8:40 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Regarding Charlie Otero and &#39;memory filling&#39; I prefer to call it memory embellishment.  It may be that his memory for his dad being worried is right on the money.  But it is possible, as Mr. Kelleher suggests, that some event was built up in his mind in an effort to &#39;make sense&#39; of such a horrible and on the surface, senseless crime.  Perhaps Mr. Otero asked him to check if an official vehicle was outside because he suspected a salesman or panhandler at the door, and did not want to answer in that case.  Such an innocent event could have become suspicious in young Charlie&#39;s mind after the murders, which would be quite understandable. <BR>I am also cruious as to if Mr. Kellher has had to opportunity to visit the crime scenes?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2166-->
